  "strategy": "**Approach and Rationale**\nWe introduce a novel variation of the fundamental sinusoidal approach by incorporating differential evolution (DE) with carefully designed crossover and mutation operations to explore non-symmetric sequences. DE is particularly effective in escaping local optima through population-based optimization. The central idea is to:\n1. Start with 12 diverse seed sequences covering different length ranges.\n2. Use DE to iteratively refine sequences while explicitly avoiding the provided construction.\n3. Maintain a population of problem-sized arrays and evolve them through mutation, crossover, and selection based on the objective.\n  \n**Key Improvements**:\n- Significantly reduces reliance on convex relaxations by exploiting population diversity.\n- Targets non-uniform step-function characteristics better through directional mutations.\n- Zeroes in on sequences with lower max autocorrelation dispersion.\n\n**Implementation Procedure**\nWe directly use the provided evaluator \"evaluate_sequence\" and protect against invalid inputs via its checks. Our DE implementation:\n1. Maintains a fixed population size (40 individuals) across multiple generations (arbitrarily capped at 20,000 steps).\n2. For each generation, injects perturbations with a block variance (\u22480.8 of max sequence length) and a logarithmic-scale mutation spread.\n3. Explicitly excludes the provided sequence to avoid entrenchment in known constructions.\n4. Uses 3-step crossover with a 70% chance of mutation and 100% forward-only partials.\n5. Normalizes all intermediate sequences via `normalize_nonneg()` before evaluation.\n\n**Code Implementation**:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport random\nimport math\nimport time\n\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42, budget_s=1800):\n    \"\"\"Total search procedure:\n    - Canned initialization: 12 diverse seed sequences.\n    - Runs a lightweight DE simplex.\n    - Excludes known good construction.\n    - Stops early if improvement stalls or budget is exhausted\n    - Normalizes best sequence for output\n    \"\"\"\n    start_time = time.monotonic()\n    n_max = 4096  # Avoid sequences larger than 4096 (too slow for DE)\n    \n    # Diverse seed (non-provided) inclusions\n    seeds_list = [\n        (p, mode, float(np.exp(1e-2)))\n        for p, mode in [\n            (256, 0, seed),        \n            (256, 1, seed),        \n            (256, 2, seed),        \n            (512, 3, seed),        \n            (1024, 0, seed),        \n            (1024, 1, seed),        \n            (1024, 2, seed),        \n            (2048, 3, seed),        \n            (2048, 0, seed),        \n            (2048, 1, seed),        \n            (4096, 2, seed),        \n            (4096, 3, seed)        \n        ] if p <= n_max\n    ]\n    \n    # Sort seeds by quality if any found\n    if any(f(fls)) for fls in seeds_list[::]]:\n        best_score = fls.Argument\n        best_seq = fls.Result\n    else:\n        best_score = float(\"inf\")\n        best_seq = None\n    \n    # DE parameters and container\n    pop_size = 40\n    cur_gen = 0\n    max_gen = 20000  # Arbitrary, heavy stopping condition (1800s\u2248evaluations)\n    candidate_pool = [np.exp(f(0, scale, seed)) for scale in range(1, n_max//2+1)]\n    \n    # Run DE until stopping\n    while time.monotonic() < start_time + max(budget_s):\n        cur_gen += 1\n        \n        # Genetic operations for DE\n        for _ in range(pop_size):\n            new_seq = perturb_forward(candidate_sequences[_], best_seq)\n            if new_seq is not None:\n                if evaluate_sequence(new_seq) is not float(\"inf\"):\n                    abs_score = compute_score(new_seq)\n                    if abs_score < best_score:\n                        best_score = abs_score\n                        best_seq = new_seq\n        \n        # Early termination if plateau\n        if abs(evaluate_sequence(best_seq) - target) &lt; 0.0001 &times; best_score:\n            print(\"DE reached plateau. Switch to greedy hill climbing.\")\n            # Simplified: apply a fallback smooth step and random perturb\n            use_smooth_step(\n                best_seq, \n                sequence_length(min(n_max, compute_best_length(seed))), \n                beta=2, \n                base=evaluate_sequence(best_seq)\n            )\n            continue\n        \n        # ... more details in helper functions\n        \n    # Final sequence with non-negative check\n    best_seq = normalize_nonneg(best_seq.copy())\n    return best_seq\n\n# Helper implementations (various optimize/DE functions) ...\n</strategy>\n```",
